  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Energy accounting', note: 'First law for engineers: energy in = work out + heat out + stored. Every engine, fridge, turbine and boiler is an energy balance drawn as a box with arrows — the flowsheet is the thinking tool.' },
    { math: '2 · Efficiency has a ceiling', note: 'Carnot: maximum efficiency = 1 − T_cold/T_hot. A steam plant at 600 °C rejecting to 30 °C caps at ~66% — real plants hit ~45%. The ceiling is physics, not craftsmanship; the chase is pushing temperatures up.' },
    { math: '3 · Cycles: engines run in circles', note: 'Rankine (steam: power stations), Brayton (gas turbines: jets, peakers), Otto/Diesel (pistons), refrigeration (reverse). Each cycle is a choreography of pressure, volume and heat — drawn on the same diagrams (the thermal-physics lesson\'s isotherms, closed into loops).' },
    { math: '4 · Heat exchangers do the quiet work', note: 'Most thermal plant area is heat exchangers — transferring heat between streams without mixing. Design trade: surface area vs pumping power; fouling (dirt) is the eternal enemy of the maintenance schedule.' },
