<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/intermediate/thermodynamics-for-engineers">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Engineering thermodynamics is the <strong>physics lesson turned into machinery</strong>: energy
    balances on control volumes, cycles that convert heat to work (and work to cooling), and
    efficiency ceilings that decide what every power plant, engine and refrigerator can ever
    achieve.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="balances, the Carnot ceiling, cycles, heat exchangers" widget="equationStep" config={thermoCfg} />

  <h2>Where the theory meets the machines</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Combined-cycle plants are Carnot\'s gift:</strong> gas turbine (Brayton) on top, its hot exhaust boiling steam (Rankine) below — efficiency ~64% (the best thermal machines ever built) by using the same fuel twice at different temperatures.</li>
    <li><strong>Heat pumps are refrigerators pointed the other way:</strong> moving heat rather than generating it — COP 3–5 (300–500% "efficiency", legal because it is transport, not creation). The building decarbonisation lesson\'s key appliance.</li>
    <li><strong>Exergy: energy quality matters:</strong> the second law refined — high-temperature heat is worth more than low; mixing hot and cold destroys usefulness irreversibly (entropy generation). Exergy analysis finds where plants waste the most.</li>
    <li><strong>Jets are Brayton with propulsion hardware:</strong> compressor → combustor → turbine (which drives the compressor) → nozzle. The afterburner dumps extra fuel in the exhaust — trading efficiency for thrust (the fluid lesson\'s nozzles).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Çengel & Boles, Thermodynamics: An Engineering Approach; Carnot (1824); the physics ladder\'s thermal-physics lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
